UBI provides a financial safety net, allowing individuals to take risks like starting a business or pursuing education, which can lead to long-term economic growth.
By reducing poverty and financial stress, UBI can increase consumer spending, which stimulates demand and drives economic activity.
UBI simplifies the welfare system, reducing administrative costs and allowing governments to invest in infrastructure and innovation that boost the economy.
Studies from pilot programs show that UBI can lead to increased employment and entrepreneurship, contributing to a more dynamic and resilient economy.
UBI can help reduce inequality by providing a baseline income, which can lead to more equitable economic participation and growth.